@end menu

@node Why OOPS?
@section Why OOPS?

EIGHTBOL's OOPS system addresses several needs for game development on
8-bit and 16-bit platforms:

@itemize @bullet
@item Memory efficiency through inheritance and shared method tables
@item Polymorphism enables uniform handling of diverse entity types
@item Encapsulation keeps slot access contained within methods
@item The COBOL-like syntax is familiar to business and game developers
@item Generated assembly is optimized for constrained hardware
@end itemize

All object instances share the same memory layout for inherited slots,
with each subclass adding its own slots at higher offsets. This allows
the runtime to pass a single pointer to any object and access any slot
through class-specific offset tables.

@node Class Hierarchy Overview
@section Class Hierarchy Overview

The class hierarchy begins with @code{Basic-Object}, an abstract base
class providing slots required by the runtime system:

@table @asis
@item id — Unique identifier (pointer-sized)
@item x — Horizontal position coordinate
@item y — Vertical position coordinate
@item layer — Display layer priority
@item flags — Boolean status flags
@item script — Associated script or behavior pointer
@end table

All other classes inherit from @code{Basic-Object}, either directly or
through intermediate classes. The hierarchy is declared using the
@code{INHERITS} clause in each class definition.

@example
CLASS-ID. Actor.
INHERITS Basic-Object.
@end example

Subclasses add their own slots and methods, and may override inherited
methods by defining a method with the same name.

@node Classes.Defs File Format
@section Classes.Defs File Format

A @file{Classes.Defs} file contains one or more class definitions,
each following this structure:

@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
CLASS-ID. ClassName.
[AUTHOR. author-name.]
[DATE-WRITTEN. date.]
[INHERITS ParentClass.]

ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

OBJECT.
    DATA DIVISION.
        WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
        @i{slot definitions}
    PROCEDURE DIVISION.
        @i{method definitions}
END OBJECT.

END CLASS ClassName.
@end example

Each class definition may include:

@table @asis
@item CLASS-ID — The class name (must be unique)
@item INHERITS — Parent class name (optional, defaults to Basic-Object)
@item DATA DIVISION — Slot (instance variable) declarations
@item PROCEDURE DIVISION — Method definitions
@end table

Comments use the COBOL convention: an asterisk (@code{*}) in column 7
marks a full-line comment.

@node Slots and Data Definitions
@section Slots and Data Definitions

Slots define the data stored in each instance of a class. Within the
OBJECT's DATA DIVISION, each slot is declared using standard COBOL
WORKING-STORAGE syntax.

@menu
* Numeric Slots::
* Array Slots::
* Character String Slots::
* BCD and Display Slots::
* Initial Values::
@end menu

@node Numeric Slots
@subsection Numeric Slots

Numeric slots store integer values and support arithmetic operations.
Define them with the PICTURE clause using 9s for digits:

@example
05  counter        PIC 9(4)    USAGE BINARY    VALUE 0.
05  score          PIC 9(6)    USAGE BINARY    VALUE 0.
05  lives-left    PIC 9       USAGE BINARY    VALUE 3.
05  speed         PIC S99     USAGE BINARY    VALUE 10.
05  position      PIC 9999    USAGE BINARY    VALUE 0.
@end example

The PICTURE clause determines the storage size:

@table @asis
@item PIC 9(n) — n decimal digits, stored in ceiling(n/2) bytes (BCD) or n bytes (BINARY)
@item PIC S9(n) — n decimal digits with sign, one extra bit (S counts as one nybble)
@end table

@node Array Slots
@subsection Array Slots

Arrays are declared with the OCCURS clause:

@example
05  high-scores.
    10  entry OCCURS 10 TIMES.
        15  initials    PIC XXX.
        15  points      PIC 9(6).
05  damage-table.
    10  damage OCCURS 32 TIMES PIC 9(4) USAGE BINARY.
@end example

Access array elements using subscripts:

@example
MOVE points OF entry(1) TO Score.
ADD 5 TO damage OF damage-table(12).
@end example

@node Character String Slots
@subsection Character String Slots

Character strings store text data using X (alphanumeric) or A
(alphabetic) PICTURE characters:

@example
05  player-name    PIC X(20)   VALUE SPACES.
05  message-text   PIC X(80)   VALUE SPACES.
05  title          PIC A(30)   VALUE SPACES.
@end example

String assignment uses the MOVE statement with STRING DELIMITED BY SIZE:

@example
STRING "Player One" DELIMITED BY SIZE
    INTO player-name.
@end example

@node BCD and Display Slots
@subsection BCD and Display Slots

BCD (Binary Coded Decimal) stores values as decimal digits, one nybble
per digit. This is useful when exact decimal representation is required:

@example
05  decimal-value  PIC 99    USAGE DECIMAL    VALUE 0.
05  score-decimal  PIC 9(6)  USAGE DECIMAL    VALUE 0.
@end example

DISPLAY usage stores values as ASCII characters:

@example
05  display-count  PIC 99    USAGE DISPLAY    VALUE 0.
@end example

@node Initial Values
@subsection Initial Values

The VALUE clause specifies the initial value for a slot. Omitted slots
contain undefined (garbage) data until explicitly assigned.

@example
05  health         PIC 9999    USAGE BINARY    VALUE 100.
05  name           PIC X(20)   VALUE SPACES.
05  flags          PIC 99      USAGE BINARY    VALUE 0.
05  multiplier     PIC 9       USAGE BINARY    VALUE 1.
@end example

Numeric literals in VALUE must match the slot's PICTURE size.

@node Method Definitions
@section Method Definitions

Methods are defined within the OBJECT's PROCEDURE DIVISION. Each method
has its own IDENTIFICATION DIVISION and PROCEDURE DIVISION.

@menu
* Method Structure::
* Method Parameters::
* Method Body Statements::
* Method Return Values::
* Special Methods::
@end menu

@node Method Structure
@subsection Method Structure

A method definition follows this pattern:

@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
METHOD-ID. "MethodName".
PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    @i{statements}
    GOBACK.
END METHOD "MethodName".
@end example

The method body contains statements in the selected frontend language.
GOBACK returns control to the caller; EXIT METHOD is also supported.

@node Method Parameters
@subsection Method Parameters

Parameters are declared after METHOD-ID using the USING clause:

@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
METHOD-ID. "TakeDamage" USING Damage-Value.
PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    SUBTRACT Damage-Value FROM HP.
    GOBACK.
END METHOD "TakeDamage".
@end example

Parameters are local to the method and do not persist in the instance.

@node Method Body Statements
@subsection Method Body Statements

The method body may contain any supported statement types. In the COBOL
frontend, these include:

@itemize @bullet
@item MOVE — Assign values to slots
@item ADD/SUBTRACT — Arithmetic operations
@item COMPUTE — Complex arithmetic expressions
@item IF/THEN/ELSE/END-IF — Conditional execution
@item PERFORM — Loops and procedure calls
@item INVOKE — Call methods on objects
@item INVOKE SUPER — Call inherited method implementation
@end itemize

Example with conditional logic:

@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
METHOD-ID. "Think".
PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    IF HP IS EQUAL TO 0 THEN
        INVOKE Self "Kill"
    ELSE
        IF HP < MaxHP THEN
            INVOKE Self "Regenerate"
        END-IF
    END-IF
    GOBACK.
END METHOD "Think".
@end example

@node Method Return Values
@subsection Method Return Values

Methods may return a value in the accumulator using RETURNING:

@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
METHOD-ID. "GetHealth" RETURNING Current-HP.
PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    MOVE HP TO Current-HP.
    GOBACK.
END METHOD "GetHealth".
@end example

For void methods (no return value), simply end with GOBACK or EXIT METHOD.

@node Special Methods
@subsection Special Methods

Certain method names have special meaning:

@table @asis
@item Think — Called every frame for game logic updates
@item Kill — Called when the entity is destroyed or removed
@item Create — Constructor-like initialization (if supported)
@end table

The Think method is particularly important; EIGHTBOL automatically
calls Think on all active entities each frame:

@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
METHOD-ID. "Think".
PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    PERFORM Update-AI.
    PERFORM Check-Bounds.
    GOBACK.
END METHOD "Think".
@end example

@node Inheritance and Polymorphism
@section Inheritance and Polymorphism

Subclasses inherit all slots and methods from their parent class.
Inheritance is declared with INHERITS:

@example
CLASS-ID. Monster.
INHERITS Actor.
@end example

The subclass automatically has access to parent's slots and methods.

@subsection Slot Inheritance

All slots from parent classes are available in the subclass. The
runtime maintains a single slot offset table for each class, so code
can access slots uniformly regardless of the actual instance type.

@subsection Method Overriding

Subclasses may override inherited methods by defining a method with the
same name. The overriding method completely replaces the inherited
implementation:

@example
* Parent class
CLASS-ID. Actor.
OBJECT.
    DATA DIVISION.
        WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
        05  HP    PIC 9999  USAGE BINARY  VALUE 100.
    PROCEDURE DIVISION.
        IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
        METHOD-ID. "TakeDamage".
        PROCEDURE DIVISION.
            SUBTRACT 10 FROM HP.
            GOBACK.
        END METHOD "TakeDamage".
END OBJECT.
END CLASS Actor.

* Subclass overrides TakeDamage
CLASS-ID. Boss.
INHERITS Actor.
OBJECT.
    PROCEDURE DIVISION.
        IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
        METHOD-ID. "TakeDamage".
        PROCEDURE DIVISION.
            SUBTRACT 5 FROM HP.
            IF HP < 1000 THEN
                INVOKE Self "Enter-Rage-Mode"
            END-IF
            GOBACK.
        END METHOD "TakeDamage".
END OBJECT.
END CLASS Boss.
@end example

@subsection Invoking Super

To call the parent class's implementation of an overridden method, use
INVOKE SUPER:

@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
METHOD-ID. "TakeDamage".
PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    INVOKE SUPER "TakeDamage".
    IF HP < MaxHP THEN
        INVOKE Self "Show-Damage-Number"
    END-IF
    GOBACK.
END METHOD "TakeDamage".
@end example

INVOKE SUPER calls the current method name on the parent class.

@node Integration with Eightbol
@section Integration with Eightbol

The OOPS system integrates with EIGHTBOL's compilation pipeline:

@menu
* Compilation Flow::
* Generated Assembly Structure::
* Virtual Method Tables::
* Copybook Generation::
@end menu

@node Compilation Flow
@subsection Compilation Flow

@enumerate
@item
Skyline-Tool processes Classes.Defs and generates slot offset tables
and copybook files (e.g., @file{*Slots.cpy})

@item
EIGHTBOL parses the frontend source code, resolving slot references
against the copybook tables

@item
The AST is built with class metadata including :class-id and :methods

@item
The backend compiles the AST to CPU-specific assembly
@end enumerate

@node Generated Assembly Structure
@subsection Generated Assembly Structure

For each class, EIGHTBOL generates:

@itemize @bullet
@item A @code{ClassName} structure definition with slot offsets
@item Method entry points with labels like @code{MethodClassNameMethodName}
@item Optional virtual method table (VMT) for polymorphic dispatch
@end itemize

Example 6502 output structure:

@example
.segment "Object"

; Structure for class Character
Character:
.word 0              ; HP (offset 0)
.word 0              ; MaxHP (offset 2)

; Method Think
MethodCharacterThink:
    ldy #HP - Character
    lda (Self), y
    ...
@end example

@node Virtual Method Tables
@subsection Virtual Method Tables

For platforms requiring polymorphic dispatch, Skyline-Tool generates
VMT structures. The VMT contains pointers to method implementations:

@example
.segment "VMT"

; Virtual Method Table for Character
CharacterVMT:
    .word MethodCharacterThink
    .word MethodCharacterKill
    .word MethodCharacterHeal
@end example

@node Copybook Generation
@subsection Copybook Generation

Skyline-Tool generates copybook files containing slot definitions for
each class. These copybooks are included in source files to declare
slots:

@example
* Phantasia-Globals-Slots.cpy — Generated copybook
* Character-Slots.cpy — Slot definitions for Character class
@end example

Including a copybook pulls in the slot declarations:

@example
COPY Phantasia-Globals-Slots.
COPY Character-Slots.
@end example

@node Example Classes.Defs
@section Example Classes.Defs

The following is a complete example showing typical class definitions:

@example
000010 IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
000020 CLASS-ID. Character.
000030 AUTHOR. Game-Developer.
000040 DATE-WRITTEN. 2026.
000050
000060 ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.
000070
000080 OBJECT.
000090     DATA DIVISION.
000100         WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
000110* Basic combat stats
000120         05  HP              PIC 9999 USAGE BINARY  VALUE 100.
000130         05  MaxHP           PIC 9999 USAGE BINARY  VALUE 100.
000140         05  Attack-Power    PIC 999  USAGE BINARY  VALUE 10.
000150         05  Defense         PIC 999  USAGE BINARY  VALUE 5.
000160
000170* State tracking
000180         05  Is-Alive        PIC 9    USAGE BINARY  VALUE 1.
000190         05  Stun-Timer      PIC 99   USAGE BINARY  VALUE 0.
000200
000210* Name storage
000220         05  Character-Name  PIC X(20) VALUE SPACES.
000230
000240* Array of recent damage taken
000250         05  Damage-History.
000260             10  Damage OCCURS 8 TIMES PIC 999 USAGE BINARY.
000270
000280     PROCEDURE DIVISION.
000290
000300         IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
000310         METHOD-ID. "Think".
000320         PROCEDURE DIVISION.
000330* Process state updates each frame
000340             IF Is-Alive IS EQUAL TO 0 THEN
000350                 INVOKE Self "Kill"
000360             END-IF
000370
000380             IF Stun-Timer > 0 THEN
000390                 SUBTRACT 1 FROM Stun-Timer
000400             END-IF
000410
000420             GOBACK.
000430         END METHOD "Think".
000440
000450         IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
000460         METHOD-ID. "TakeDamage" USING Damage.
000470         PROCEDURE DIVISION.
000480* Apply damage reduction from defense
000490             COMPUTE Temporary = Damage * (100 - Defense) / 100
000500             SUBTRACT Temporary FROM HP
000510
000520* Record damage in history (shift register)
000530             PERFORM Shift-Damage-History
000540             MOVE Damage TO Damage OF Damage-History(1)
000550
000560             IF HP < 0 THEN
000570                 MOVE 0 TO HP
000580                 MOVE 0 TO Is-Alive
000590             END-IF
000600
000610             GOBACK.
000620         END METHOD "TakeDamage".
000630
000640         IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
000650         METHOD-ID. "Heal" USING Amount.
000660         PROCEDURE DIVISION.
000670             ADD Amount TO HP
000680             IF HP > MaxHP THEN
000690                 MOVE MaxHP TO HP
000700             END-IF
000710             GOBACK.
000720         END METHOD "Heal".
000730
000740         IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
000750         METHOD-ID. "Kill".
000760         PROCEDURE DIVISION.
000770             MOVE 0 TO HP
000780             MOVE 0 TO Is-Alive
000790             GOBACK.
000800         END METHOD "Kill".
000810
000820 END OBJECT.
000830
000840 END CLASS Character.
@end example

This example demonstrates:

@itemize @bullet
@item Numeric slots with BINARY usage and initial values
@item Array slots with OCCURS
@item Character string slots
@item Methods with parameters (TakeDamage, Heal)
@item Methods without parameters (Think, Kill)
@item Conditional logic with IF/THEN/ELSE
@item Self-invocation for internal method calls
@item Arithmetic with COMPUTE
@end itemize

